:"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":442.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":184.12,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":247.32,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1065.2,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1063.84,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":272.16,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":181.32,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":466.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":26.88,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":716.28,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":743.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1639.24,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":2.64,"topicId":"interaction-centre","topicLabel":"INTERACTION CENTRE"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":568.32,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":672,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":761.16,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"}]},"New Society":{"slug":"new-society","body":"New Society was a weekly magazine of social inquiry and social and cultural comment, published in the United Kingdom from 1962 to 1988. It drew on the disciplines of sociology, anthropology, psychology, human geography, social history and social policy, and it published wide-ranging social reportage. The magazine was launched by a small, London-based independent publishing house, Harrison Raison, which in 1956 had successfully launched <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Scientist\">New Scientist</a>, a weekly magazine to serve the natural sciences. The idea was to create a comparable magazine about the social sciences. The cultural commentator <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Hewison\">Robert Hewison</a> wrote that New Society became \"a forum for the new <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelligentsia\">intelligentsia</a>\", crested by the expansion of higher education in Britain from the early 1960s. New Society was usually perceived as centre-left, but it was fiercely non-partisan and never endorsed any political party. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timothy_Raison\">Timothy Raison</a> (1962–68), its founding editor, was later a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservative_Party_(UK)\">Conservative</a> MP from 1970 until 1992. In Raison's opinion, New Society sought \"to mirror, to analyse, to understand, not to exhort or moralise.\" It tried to see the world as it was, not as it was supposed to be. These aims were continued and developed under the editorship of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Barker_(writer)\">Paul Barker</a> (1968–86), who was described by the labour historian <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Hobsbawm\">Eric Hobsbawm</a> as the \"most original of editors\".","subtitle":"Magazine","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/new-society.jpg","external":"http://www.timeshighereducation.co.uk/162372.article","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.5073509,-0.1277583,7z","also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1329.44,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":2116.64,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":29.6,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":146.16,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":1181.08,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"}]},"Parliament":{"slug":"parliament","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"text-align: justify; text-justify: inter-ideograph; mso-pagination: none; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;\">The Parliament of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland, commonly known as the UK Parliament, is the supreme <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legislature\">legislative body</a> in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom\">United Kingdom</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crown_dependencies\">British Crown dependencies</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_overseas_territories\">British overseas territories</a>. It alone possesses <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliamentary_sovereignty\">legislative supremacy</a> and thereby ultimate power over all other political bodies in the UK and its territories. Its head is the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monarchy_of_the_United_Kingdom\">Sovereign of the United Kingdom</a> (currently <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_II_of_the_United_Kingdom\">Queen Elizabeth II</a>) and its seat is the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace_of_Westminster\">Palace of Westminster</a> in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westminster\">Westminster</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London\">London</a>.</p>","subtitle":"Parliament of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/parliament.jpg","external":"http://www.parliament.uk","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/search/parliament+uk/@51.499554,-0.124998,17z","also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":2131.68,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":304.52,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"}]},"Paul Barker":{"slug":"paul-barker","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Barker was educated at local schools in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calder_Valley\">Calder Valley</a> and won an <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exhibition_(scholarship)\">Exhibition (scholarship)</a> to <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brasenose_College,_Oxford\">Brasenose College, Oxford</a>, to read French. Before taking up his place at Oxford, he did <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_service\">National service</a> and was commissioned as an officer in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelligence_Corps_(United_Kingdom)\">Intelligence Corps</a>, and while in the Army studied <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_language\">Russian language</a> at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cambridge_University\">Cambridge University</a>. After taking his Oxford degree, he then went on to the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89cole_Normale_Sup%C3%A9rieure\">École Normale Supérieure</a> in Paris for a year as lecteur. He joined the London staff of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Times\">The Times</a> in 1959, but early in 1964 left to join the recently founded <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Society\">New Society</a> as a staff writer. He went on to <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Economist\">The Economist</a>, but returned to New Society almost at once - in 1965 - as deputy editor. In 1968 he succeeded <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timothy_Raison\">Timothy Raison</a>, the first editor of New Society, and edited the magazine until 1986. Subsequently, he was a columnist on <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sunday_Times\">The Sunday Times</a> and a regular writer for the London <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evening_Standard\">Evening Standard</a>, the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Times_Literary_Supplement\">Times Literary Supplement</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prospect_Magazine\">Prospect Magazine</a>. He is currently a senior research fellow with the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.youngfoundation.org.uk/\">Young Foundation</a>, as well as being a freelance journalist, broadcaster and author.</p>","subtitle":"Journalist and writer","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/paul-barker.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Barker_(writer)","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK","also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1331.12,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":2117.92,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":1183.68,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"}]},"Pop-up Parliament":{"slug":"pop-up-parliament","body":"It was announced this week that the statues of former prime ministers that stand in the House of Commons, the lower house of the British parliament, are to be protected from members of Parliament who touch them for luck. According to Melanie Unwin, deputy curator of the Houses of Parliament, “there are now cracks and small holes on the surface of the [Winston] Churchill statue and substantial loss of surface texture on other statues.” In 1965, Price proposed a Pop-up Parliament to replace the existing one. \"If we want an efficient parliament, let’s give it a whole efficient building to work . . . replace the present historic monument with an up-to-date structure – flexible, accessible and dispensable,” he wrote.","subtitle":"Cedric Price","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/pop-up-parliament.jpg","external":"http://www.cca.qc.ca/en/collection/2166-a-pop-up-parliament","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.5073509,-0.1277583,7z","also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":496,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1589.88,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1896.04,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":2111.12,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":377.84,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"}]},"Winston Churchill":{"slug":"winston-churchill","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"text-align: justify; text-justify: inter-ideograph; mso-pagination: none; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;\">Sir Winston Leonard Spencer-Churchill <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knight_of_the_Order_of_the_Garter\">KG</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Member_of_the_Order_of_Merit\">OM</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Companion_of_Honour\">CH</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_Decoration\">TD</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deputy_Lieutenant\">DL</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fellow_of_the_Royal_Society\">FRS</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Academician\">RA</a> (30 November 1874 – 24 January 1965) was a British <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Politician\">politician</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobel_Prize\">Nobel laureate</a> who was the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_Minister_of_the_United_Kingdom\">Prime Minister of the United Kingdom</a> from 1940 to 1945 and again from 1951 to 1955. Widely regarded as one of the greatest wartime leaders of the 20th century, Churchill was also an <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Officer_(armed_forces)\">officer</a> in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Army\">British Army</a>, a historian, a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winston_Churchill_as_writer\">writer (as Winston S. Churchill)</a>, and an artist. Churchill is the only British Prime Minister to have won the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobel_Prize_in_Literature\">Nobel Prize in Literature</a> since its inception in 1901, and was the first person to be made an <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honorary_citizen_of_the_United_States\">honorary citizen of the United States</a>.</p>","subtitle":"British politician","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/winston-churchill.jpg","external":"http://www.winstonchurchill.org","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Hyde+Park+Gate","also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":2148.56,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"}]}}}